# **The Business Context**
 
There is an urgent requirement to get a first regime live. A specific government department, on Salesforce, needs a working citizen data-gathering journey — one complete regime, end to end, accessible to real citizens — within three months. That is the immediate deliverable. It is not negotiable on timeline.
 
But the first regime is not the destination. It is the proof of value that unlocks the full programme: many departments, many regimes, cross-department pre-population, and the network effects described in the Vision Paper. The architecture is explicitly designed to support that scale. The question this document addresses is how to get from the urgent first delivery to the scalable platform without wasting effort, losing momentum, or asking the programme to absorb a long gap between demonstrable value and full capability.
 
The answer is a deliberate two-phase plan. Phase 1 delivers the first regime live in three months. Phase 2 converts that single-org implementation into a distributable managed package and completes the platform tooling, in a further three months. Together they deliver a full production platform in six months, with working citizen value demonstrated at month three.
 
This document describes both phases in detail and then, critically, assesses the cost of acting in two phases rather than building the full platform in a single longer programme. The conclusion — argued in the Assessment section — is that the nugatory work involved is small, the risk reduction is significant, and the architecture is specifically designed to make two-phase delivery efficient.
 
*This document should be read after the Vision Paper, Functional Architecture, and Implementation Options paper. It assumes familiarity with the three-tier model, the managed package architecture, and the Salesforce implementation pattern described in those documents.*
 
**Phase 1***  —  First Regime Live — Three Months*
 
## **What Phase 1 Delivers**
 
Phase 1 delivers a single, complete, citizen-facing regime on Salesforce Experience Cloud, within a single department Salesforce org, accessible to real citizens within three months of the team starting work. Specifically:
 
- One fully-configured regime, end to end: citizen login via GOV.UK One Login or equivalent; the complete question journey including conditional routing, question sets, and a check-your-answers confirmation page; answer storage with full history; and a confirmed submission.
 
- Section status tracking and resumable journeys: a citizen who starts and does not finish can return and continue from where they left off.
 
- Basic permission model: citizens can access their own regimes; agents can be granted access to act on behalf of a citizen.
 
- Pre-population within the regime: where the citizen has previously answered a question in another section of the same regime, the answer is offered as a suggestion in the new context.
 
- Accessibility to WCAG 2.1 AA on the core citizen journey: question pages, check your answers, confirmation.
 
- A passing automated test suite covering the routing engine, answer storage, and permission logic.
 
- A clean, well-documented single-org Salesforce codebase that is explicitly structured for managed package conversion in Phase 2.
 
Phase 1 deliberately defers:
 
- Managed package architecture, namespacing, and upgrade paths.
 
- The Salesforce-based regime configuration wizard. Regime configuration in Phase 1 uses CSV/CLI tooling — a direct port of the proof-of-concept tooling — operated by the platform team on behalf of the department.
 
- The full delegation chain model (citizen delegating to a colleague or professional intermediary). Basic agent access is included; the full chain-of-delegation audit is Phase 2.
 
- Cross-department pre-population. The consent token mechanism and inter-org API call are designed but not implemented.
 
- Table sections, if not required by the first regime. If the first regime requires repeating-row data entry, this is included; if not, it is deferred.
 
*Phase 1 is not a prototype or a pilot. It is a production deployment of a real regime, used by real citizens. The deferrals are additive capabilities that do not exist yet; they do not remove or degrade anything that is built.*
 
## **The Phase 1 Team**
 
Phase 1 requires a dedicated team of four. All four must be available full-time for the three months; part-time or shared resources are not compatible with this timeline.
 
| **Role** | **Key competencies required** | **Primary responsibilities** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Senior Salesforce Architect** | Managed package architecture (for Phase 2 readiness); LWC component design; Apex service design; Platform Cache strategy; GOV.UK One Login integration; technical leadership | Overall technical direction; routing engine; session management; inter-component architecture; Phase 2 readiness decisions |
| **Mid-level LWC Specialist** | Lightning Web Components; GDS/GOV.UK Frontend; sfGpsDsUkGov library (if Omnistudio available); WCAG 2.1 AA implementation; Experience Cloud configuration | Full LWC component library build; accessibility implementation; Experience Cloud site configuration |
| **Apex and Data Developer** | Apex triggers and services; Custom Objects and Custom Metadata; SOQL optimisation; governor limit analysis; test class coverage | Answer storage and history service; permission model; section status tracking; CSV/CLI configuration tooling; test suite |
| **Tester and Accessibility Specialist** | Manual and automated testing; WCAG 2.1 AA audit; screen reader testing; government service assessment criteria; UAT coordination | Test planning and execution from week 4; accessibility audit from week 8; UAT support; defect management |
 
The architect must have prior experience with Salesforce managed package architecture, even though the package is not built in Phase 1. Decisions made in Phase 1 about namespacing conventions, object structure, and code organisation will either enable or impede Phase 2. An architect without managed package experience will make Phase 1 decisions that create avoidable Phase 2 rework.
 
