## **Phase 1 Timeline**
 
The three tracks — routing engine, LWC component library, and Apex services — run in parallel from week 2, after the org setup and data model are complete. This parallelism is what makes three months achievable.
 
- **Weeks 1–2: **Org setup, data model, naming conventions, Component design review. All four team members.
 
- **Weeks 3–8: **Routing engine (Architect); LWC component library (LWC Specialist + Architect support); Apex services — answer, status, permission (Data Developer). Three parallel tracks.
 
- **Weeks 7–9: **Session management and One Login integration (Architect); regime configuration (Data Developer); tester joins full-time.
 
- **Weeks 9–11: **Integration, end-to-end testing, accessibility audit, remediation.
 
- **Weeks 11–12: **UAT, performance profiling, go-live preparation.
 
The critical path is the LWC component library. It is the largest single item and cannot be meaningfully parallelised beyond two people. If the sfGpsDsUkGov library is available and compatible with the host org’s Omnistudio version, the LWC track compresses from six weeks to three to four. Confirming Omnistudio availability is the first action for the team, before any other work begins.
 
**Phase 2***  —  Managed Package and Full Platform — Three Further Months*
 
## **What Phase 2 Delivers**
 
Phase 2 converts the single-org Phase 1 implementation into a distributable Salesforce managed package and completes the platform capabilities deferred from Phase 1. At the end of Phase 2, the platform is ready for any participating department to install, configure their own regimes, and go live without bespoke build work.
 
Specifically, Phase 2 delivers:
 
- A Salesforce managed package containing the complete platform: data model, routing engine, Apex services, LWC component library, permission model, session management, and configuration tooling.
 
- Namespace applied throughout, with upgrade path design and testing validated against at least two test orgs with different configurations.
 
- A Salesforce-based regime configuration wizard: a staff-facing LWC application that guides a department through defining a new regime, granting permissions, and generating a working regime home page, without developer involvement.
 
- The full delegation chain model: citizens delegating to professional intermediaries, with chain-of-delegation audit trail.
 
- Cross-department pre-population: the consent token mechanism, the inter-org API call, and the central service registry of department endpoints and public keys.
 
- Table sections, if deferred from Phase 1.
 
- A second regime configured and live, demonstrating that the platform generalises beyond the first customer.
 
- Complete documentation: installation guide for new departments, regime configuration guide, upgrade guide, and a technical reference for the Apex service layer.
 
## **The Phase 2 Team**
 
Phase 2 requires a dedicated team of three. The tester from Phase 1 continues part-time for upgrade testing; a full-time fourth seat is not required because the heavy UI build is complete.
 
